WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.125
Let's make the target concrete.

00:00:02.575 --> 00:00:03.729
Start with prices.

00:00:04.179 --> 00:00:08.237
The log return is the log of today's price minus the log of yesterday's.

00:00:08.687 --> 00:00:15.447
Here are real closing values from South Africa in early January 2012, and the returns they produce.

00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:19.926
Returns are small numbers, a fraction of a percent on most days.

00:00:20.376 --> 00:00:23.333
Now take the average of the last twenty returns.

00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:25.100
Call it r bar twenty.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.815
Tomorrow's return will land somewhere.

00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:31.450
Its distance from that recent average is the surprise.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.358
Square the surprise and you get our target, y t plus one.

00:00:35.808 --> 00:00:40.863
It is always positive, and it is large exactly on the days the market moves a lot.

00:00:41.313 --> 00:00:45.790
This squared deviation is a noisy stand-in for the true, hidden variance.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:55.766
Every model in this video produces a forecast, y hat, of this same quantity, and we score them all by root mean squared error on days they never saw.
